deep.reconstruction:
Setting (from this page and its neighbours). Σ is a finite arrangement of pseudo-lines D_i in the real projective plane, and L is one further pseudo-line in some 'position relative' to Σ. Cutting RP² along L gives a closed disc Δ = Δ_L. Its boundary ∂Δ = L̃ is the double cover of L, with antipodal points over the same point of L. Each D_i becomes a chord d_i with antipodal endpoints. Two chords are disjoint ('parallel') exactly when the corresponding D_i meet on L, i.e. when L passes through a vertex S of Σ.

The previous page characterises which chord systems arise this way: chords cross at most once, and parallelism is an equivalence relation whose classes C have their 2ν endpoints forming two antipodal runs of ν consecutive points. It also defines the generalization arcs: for a parallel class C (L through a vertex S of Σ), the arcs of L̃ cut out by the endpoint runs S', S''. Moving L off S 'generalises' its position.

This page defines ordinary specialization arcs. Take a set σ of points on L̃ with card σ ≥ 2 such that:
(a) σ forms an arc of consecutive endpoints, lying on one side of the complement of the chords;
(b) the chords through the points s ∈ σ all pass through one interior vertex t (necessarily unique), and every chord through t is among them;
(c) no s ∈ σ lies on a generalization arc, i.e. no s is a vertex of Σ.
Then, for non-trivial Σ:
- for each s there is a unique chord arc [t, s];
- for adjacent s, s' there is a unique cell, a triangle t s s';
- the union of these triangles is a fan with apex t, called the specialization triangle;
- its base, the arc of L̃ intercepted by σ, is an ordinary specialization arc.
Geometrically, L can be swept across the vertex t, passing through t at the intermediate moment (specialisation to a position where L contains a vertex of Σ). This is the non-simple version of a Ringel triangle flip involving L. All these arcs, general and special, are claimed to be mutually disjoint.

Exceptional specialization arcs come next, as the cut-off right column reconstructs. They are tied to the situation where L passes through a vertex of Σ and can degenerate further, apparently towards coinciding with a line D of Σ. The relevant arc of L̃ has both endpoints in one parallel class C₁ (the chords D₁). It is therefore not an ordinary specialization arc, and it meets no ordinary specialization arc and hence no generalization arc.

The next page completes the picture. The 'transition vertices' cut L̃ into generalization, specialization (ordinary or exceptional) and 'inaction' arcs, the sides of 'characteristic polygons', with adjacency rules. Overall the page is a local combinatorial description of the neighbours of a given extension Σ ∪ {L} in the space of all positions of L: the extension space of a rank-3 oriented matroid. Moves are flips through vertices (specialization ↔ generalization).
